E=[
(1,1859,"Cosmos","Carrington sees two star-like objects cross the Sun","On the day of the great solar flare, Richard Carrington sees two star-like objects cross the Sun. Fort lists the observation among his unexplained solar transits. Monthly Notices of the R.A.S. published it.","NL","New Lands, citing Monthly Notices, 20-13, 15, 88","Sept. 1, 1859-two star-like objects that were seen by Carrington to cross the sun"),
(2,1786,"Sky","A ball of fire that lasted forty minutes","A bright ball of fire and light is seen in a hurricane in England, visible for forty minutes, about 800 times longer than the orthodox duration of a meteor. Fort cites Greg's Catalogues and the Annual Register.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Greg's Catalogues","bright ball of fire and light in a hurricane in England, Sept. 2, 1786"),
(4,1873,"Falls","The black rain at Marlsford","A black rain falls at Marlsford, England, and more than 24 hours later another falls in the same small town. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 9-43","a black rain fell at Marlsford, England, Sept. 4, 1873"),
(5,1865,"Cosmos","A bright spot west of Picard","Ingall sees a conspicuous bright spot west of the lunar crater Picard. He sees it again on the 7th, but on the 8th it is gone, replaced by a cloud-like effect.","NL","New Lands, citing Astronomical Register, 3-252","Sept. 5, 1865-a conspicuous bright spot west of Picard"),
(6,1835,"Falls","A meteorite that left jelly at Gotha","A meteorite falls at Gotha, Germany, 'leaving a jelly-like mass on the ground', only three feet from an observer. Greg records it without comment. Fort uses it against explanations of star-jelly as spawn.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Greg","fall of a meteorite at Gotha, Germany, Sept. 6, 1835"),
(7,1820,"Sky","Objects marching over Embrun","During the eclipse, many people in the streets of Embrun, France see objects moving in straight lines, turning and retracing their paths, separated by uniform spaces. Fort cites Arago's Oeuvres.","NL","New Lands, citing Arago, OEuvres, 11-576","objects that were seen by many persons, in the streets of Embrun, during the eclipse of Sept. 7, 1820"),
(8,1893,"Mysteries","An explosive sound over the Channel","Prof. Gerard hears an explosive sound over the English Channel near Dover, one of a run of unexplained detonations Fort traced across Belgium and the Channel.","NL","New Lands, citing Prof. Gerard","Sept. 8, 1893"),
(9,1848,"Haunts","A regiment in the sky over Lough Foyle","In the afternoon at Quigley's Point on Lough Foyle, the sky turns dark and seems to open, showing a reddish area in which a regiment of soldiers appears. The Times reported it on the 13th.","NL","New Lands, citing London Times, Sept. 13, 1848","Afternoon of Sept. 9, 1848"),
(10,1891,"Sky","Prof. Copeland's moving object","Prof. Copeland describes an object he saw moving across the sky. Dreyer says he had seen the same object at the Armagh Observatory. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 44-519, 541","Prof. Copeland describes a similar appearance that he had seen, Sept. 10, 1891"),
(11,1891,"Sky","Alexander Graham Bell's object","Dr. Alexander Graham Bell sees in Nova Scotia the moving object that Copeland and Dreyer also reported. Fort uses it to rebuke the 'Old Dominant', who dismissed earlier sightings of the same sort.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 44-541","It was seen by Dr. Alexander Graham Bell, Sept. 11, 1891, in Nova Scotia"),
(13,1889,"Cosmos","A white-bordered black spot in Plinius","Prof. Thury of Geneva reports an appearance in the lunar crater Plinius, a black spot with an 'intensely white' border. Fort lists it with new black spots in Copernicus and Gassendi that year.","NL","New Lands, citing Prof. Thury","An appearance in Plinius, Sept. 13, 1889"),
(14,1908,"Sky","A light from an unseen moon at Gosport","At Gosport, Hampshire, a light is seen that comes as if from an unseen moon. Fort suggests a moon-like object crossed the Midlands and south of England that night. Country Queries and Notes reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing Country Queries and Notes, 1-138, 417","night of Sept. 14, 1908, was seen a light that came as if from an unseen moon"),
(15,1859,"Cosmos","Tice's object over St. Louis","John H. Tice of St. Louis reports an unknown object. Fort says Tice was said not to be trustworthy 'but who has any way of knowing?'","NL","New Lands, on John H. Tice","reported by John H. Tice, of St. Louis, Mo., Sept. 15, 1859"),
(17,1885,"Falls","An aerolite with no trace","An aerolite falls in a storm at St. Leonards-on-Sea, England, and no trace of it can be found. Fort cites the Annual Register among his cases of meteorites in storms.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Annual Register, 1885","Aerolite in a storm at St. Leonards-on-sea, England, Sept. 17, 1885"),
(20,1839,"Falls","Fish fall in a straight line near Calcutta","About 20 miles south of Calcutta live fish fall and some survive in a tank. A witness says they fell not helter-skelter but in a straight line no more than a cubit wide. Flammarion tells of it in The Atmosphere.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Flammarion, The Atmosphere, p. 414","India, about 20 miles south of Calcutta, Sept. 20, 1839"),
(21,1741,"Falls","Cobwebs fall at Selborne","A substance described as cobwebs falls in flakes over Bradly, Selborne and Alresford, England, in a triangle bounded by the three towns. Fort cites All the Year Round.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing All the Year Round, 8-254","a fall that took place in England, Sept. 21, 1741, in the towns of Bradly, Selborne, and Alresford"),
(24,1883,"Cosmos","Hicks Pasha's black spot on the Sun","Writing to the Times, Hicks Pasha says that in Egypt he saw through glasses 'an immense black spot upon the lower part of the sun'. Fort puts it in the extraordinary year of 1883.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Dec. 17, 1883","in Egypt, Sept. 24, 1883, he had seen, through glasses"),
(25,1893,"Cosmos","A shaft of light from the Moon","Mr. Gaboreau at Paris sees a shaft of light projecting from the Moon, like the one de Moraes saw in the Azores that April. L'Astronomie reported both.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Astronomie, 13-34","A similar appearance was seen, Sept. 25, 1893, at Paris"),
(26,1916,"Falls","A thunderstone at Cardiff","A 'thunderstone' is said to have fallen at Cardiff. Nature said it was coincidence and only a lightning flash had been seen. Fort cites the Times.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Sept. 28, 1916; Nature, 98-95","said to have fallen at Cardiff, Sept. 26, 1916"),
(27,1846,"Haunts","A city in the sky of Liverpool","A city is seen in the sky over Liverpool. The explanation is that it was a mirage of Edinburgh. Fort thinks the identification was suggested by what the observers had already been told.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1847-39","a city in the sky of Liverpool"),
(29,1885,"Sky","Animals and dancers in the clouds at Valla","In the evening at Valla, Sweden, a lurid glare on the northwestern horizon shows a cloud bank that forms animals, groups of dancers, a forest and a park with paths. Fort puts it with the Swedish sky-pictures.","NL","New Lands, on the Swedish mirages","Sept. 29, 1885-again at Valla"),
(30,1886,"Sky","A moon-sized light over Iloilo","At Iloilo, in the Philippines, a luminous object the size of the full Moon floats slowly northward, followed by smaller ones close to it. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 35-173","at Yloilo, Sept. 30, 1886, was seen a luminous object the size of the full moon"),
]
